### What is the fundamental tension?

A feed read is extremely frequent and must be fast. Posting is rare but can affect millions of followers. You are trading where you pay the cost.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

public class FeedTension {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Feed design tension:");
        System.out.println("  Reads: millions per second, must be < 50ms");
        System.out.println("  Writes: thousands per second, can tolerate seconds");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  One post by user with 500 followers: 500 writes (manageable)");
        System.out.println("  One post by celebrity with 5M followers: 5,000,000 writes (!)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Trade-off: pay at write time or at read time?");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Feed design tension:
  Reads: millions per second, must be < 50ms
  Writes: thousands per second, can tolerate seconds
  One post by celebrity with 5M followers: 5,000,000 writes!
```

### How does fanout-on-write (push) work?

At post time, push a denormalized entry into each follower's precomputed timeline. Reading is a simple list lookup.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

import java.util.*;

public class FanoutOnWrite {
    private final Map<String, List<String>> timelines = new HashMap<>();

    public void post(String author, String content, List<String> followers) {
        String entry = author + ": " + content;
        for (String follower : followers) {
            timelines.computeIfAbsent(follower, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(0, entry);
        }
        System.out.println("Posted by " + author + " -> pushed to " + followers.size() + " timelines");
    }

    public List<String> readFeed(String userId) {
        return timelines.getOrDefault(userId, List.of());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var feed = new FanoutOnWrite();
        feed.post("analyst-pankaj", "NBI Q4 results: strong growth",
                List.of("rajesh", "priya", "vikram"));
        System.out.println("Rajesh's feed: " + feed.readFeed("rajesh"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Posted by analyst-pankaj -> pushed to 3 timelines
Rajesh's feed: [analyst-pankaj: NBI Q4 results: strong growth]

Pro: read is O(1) list lookup
Con: write amplification = follower count
```

### How does fanout-on-read (pull) work?

Store posts once. On read, pull and merge the followees' recent posts.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

import java.util.*;

public class FanoutOnRead {
    private final Map<String, List<String>> posts = new HashMap<>();
    private final Map<String, List<String>> follows = new HashMap<>();

    public void post(String author, String content) {
        posts.computeIfAbsent(author, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(0, content);
        System.out.println("Stored post by " + author + " (1 write, no fanout)");
    }

    public List<String> readFeed(String userId) {
        List<String> feed = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String followee : follows.getOrDefault(userId, List.of())) {
            List<String> followeePosts = posts.getOrDefault(followee, List.of());
            if (!followeePosts.isEmpty()) {
                feed.add(followee + ": " + followeePosts.get(0));
            }
        }
        return feed;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var feed = new FanoutOnRead();
        feed.follows.put("rajesh", List.of("analyst-pankaj", "market-index"));
        feed.post("analyst-pankaj", "NBI Q4 results: strong growth");
        feed.post("market-index", "Sensex up 2% today");
        System.out.println("Rajesh's feed: " + feed.readFeed("rajesh"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Stored post by analyst-pankaj (1 write, no fanout)
Stored post by market-index (1 write, no fanout)
Rajesh's feed: [analyst-pankaj: NBI Q4 results, market-index: Sensex up 2%]

Pro: write is O(1)
Con: read is O(followees) fan-in merge -> slow at scale
```

### Why does neither pure strategy work?

Pure push collapses on celebrities. Pure pull collapses on normal reads.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

public class WhyNeitherWorks {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pure fanout-on-write:");
        System.out.println("  Normal user (500 followers): 500 writes -> fine");
        System.out.println("  Celebrity (5M followers): 5,000,000 writes -> CATASTROPHIC");
        System.out.println("  Write storm, slow, expensive, wastes effort on inactive followers");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Pure fanout-on-read:");
        System.out.println("  Every feed load: pull + merge from all followees -> SLOW");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh follows 200 people -> merge 200 post lists on every read");
        System.out.println("  Millions of reads -> massive fan-in load");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Solution: HYBRID -> push for normal, pull for celebrities");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Pure push: celebrity = 5M writes per post -> catastrophic
Pure pull: every read = merge 200 lists -> slow
Solution: HYBRID
```

### How does the hybrid work?

Normal users: push into followers' timelines. Celebrities: pull at read time and merge with precomputed timeline.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

public class HybridFeed {
    private static final int CELEBRITY_THRESHOLD = 10000;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int analystFollowers = 500;
        int indexFollowers = 5_000_000;

        System.out.println("Hybrid feed:");
        System.out.println("  analyst-pankaj (" + analystFollowers + " followers): PUSH");
        System.out.println("    -> Push into 500 timelines. Read: O(1) list lookup.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  market-index (" + indexFollowers + " followers): PULL");
        System.out.println("    -> Store once. On read: merge cached celebrity posts.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Rajesh's feed read:");
        System.out.println("    1. Read precomputed timeline (analyst posts already there)");
        System.out.println("    2. Pull cached market-index recent posts");
        System.out.println("    3. Merge and sort by time");
        System.out.println("    = Fast reads + no celebrity write storm");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Hybrid feed:
  analyst-pankaj (500 followers): PUSH into 500 timelines
  market-index (5000000 followers): PULL at read time

  Rajesh's feed: precomputed + merge celebrity cache
  = Fast reads + no celebrity write storm
```

### What supporting details make this production-grade?

Cache celebrity posts. Cap timelines. Async fanout. Dedup.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

public class ProductionDetails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Production details:");
        System.out.println("  1. Cache celebrity recent posts (many followers pull same posts)");
        System.out.println("  2. Cap timeline size to 500 entries (rarely scrolled far)");
        System.out.println("  3. Async fanout via queue (posting returns immediately)");
        System.out.println("  4. Denormalized entries (no joins at read time)");
        System.out.println("  5. Deduplication on merge");
        System.out.println("  6. Celebrity threshold from real follower distribution");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Production details:
  1. Cache celebrity posts (many followers pull same)
  2. Cap timelines to 500 (bound storage)
  3. Async fanout (posting returns fast)
  4. Denormalized entries (no joins)
  5. Dedup on merge
  6. Threshold from real data
```

### How do you handle edge cases?

New follow backfill, deleted posts, celebrity threshold crossing.

```java
package com.bank.feed;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Edge cases:");
        System.out.println("  New follow: backfill last N posts into timeline");
        System.out.println("  Deleted post: store references, check visibility at read");
        System.out.println("  Crossing threshold: user gains 10K followers -> switch to pull");
        System.out.println("  Inactive users: fanout wastes effort -> skip or lazy-load");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Edge cases:
  New follow: backfill last N posts
  Deleted post: check visibility at read
  Crossing threshold: switch strategy
  Inactive users: skip or lazy-load
```

### How does the approach compare?

| Strategy | Write Cost | Read Cost | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|
| Fanout-on-write | O(followers) | O(1) | Normal users |
| Fanout-on-read | O(1) | O(followees) | Celebrities |
| Hybrid | Mixed | Fast for both | Production systems |

```text
The hybrid exists to pay the cost where it is cheapest for each class of user.
  Normal: cheap push (few followers), instant read
  Celebrity: cheap store (one write), merge at read with caching
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The celebrity threshold trades write cost against read cost. Revisit it from the actual follower distribution. Store denormalized entries but reference canonical posts for edits and deletes.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Hybrid: push for normal, pull for celebrity
  [x] Async fanout via queue
  [x] Celebrity threshold from real follower distribution
  [x] Cache celebrity posts
  [x] Cap timelines (bound storage and latency)
  [x] Denormalized entries for fast reads
  [x] Handle: new follows, deletes, threshold crossing
```
